Mysh - A Simple Custom Shell
Mysh is a lightweight, custom shell implementation written in C. It provides basic shell functionality such as executing commands, handling input/output redirection, pipelines, and wildcard expansion, along with a set of built-in commands.

Features
Built-in Commands:

cd: Change the current working directory.
help: Display information about available built-in commands.
exit: Exit the shell.
which: Identify the location of an executable or built-in command.
pwd: Print the current working directory.
External Commands:

Executes commands from the system's /usr/bin, /usr/local/bin, and /bin directories.
Input/Output Redirection:

Redirect input using < and output using >.
Pipeline Support:

Commands can be chained using | to create pipelines.
Wildcard Expansion:

Supports * for pattern matching and expands file paths accordingly.
Interactive and Batch Modes:

Interactive Mode: Type commands directly in the shell.
Batch Mode: Execute commands from a script file.
Installation
Clone the repository or copy the source code into a file named mysh.c.
Compile the code using gcc: gcc -o mysh mysh.c
Run the shell:
Interactive Mode: ./mysh
Batch Mode: ./mysh <batch_file>
Usage
Interactive Mode
Once launched, the shell will display a prompt (mysh>). Enter commands just as you would in any other shell.

Example:

mysh> pwd
mysh> cd /path/to/directory  //No single or double quotation marks needed
mysh> ls | grep file
Built-in Commands
cd: Change directory.
mysh> cd /path/to/directory
help: List available built-in commands.
mysh> help
exit: Exit the shell.
mysh> exit
which: Locate an executable or a built-in command.
mysh> which ls
pwd: Print the current working directory.
mysh> pwd
Input/Output Redirection
Redirect input:

mysh> sort < input.txt
Redirect output:

mysh> ls > output.txt
Pipelines
Chain commands with pipes:

mysh> ls | grep ".c"
Wildcard Expansion
Use * to match filenames:

mysh> echo *.c
File Structure
Main Functions:

main: Entry point for interactive or batch mode.
mysh_loop: Core loop handling user input and command execution.
Command Handling:

mysh_execute: Determines if a command is built-in or external.
mysh_launch: Executes external commands.
handle_pipeline: Handles commands separated by |.
handle_redirection: Manages < and > operators.
Utility Functions:

mysh_read_line_read: Reads input line-by-line.
mysh_split_line: Tokenizes input commands.
expand_wildcards: Expands file patterns like *.


            Test Cases
List Files in Directory

Command: ls
Expected Output: List of files and directories in the current directory.
Pipeline with Sorting

Command: ls | sort
Expected Output: List of files and directories in sorted order.
Redirect Output

Command: ls > output.txt
Expected Output: Creates a file named output.txt containing the list of files and directories in the current directory.
Invalid Command

Command: paspd
Expected Output: mysh: paspd: command not found
Change Directory to Parent

Command: cd ..
Expected Output: Moves to the parent directory. The command ls afterward confirms the contents of the parent directory.
Help Command

Command: help
Expected Output:
bash
